@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@ This chapter describes the application, experimental design, procedure, and data
 
 The application runs on the HTC VIVE Focus Vision headset. Each participant completes one practice trial and 80 measured trials, divided into two counterbalanced blocks of 40 trials, one per voice condition. The project source is available at \href{https://github.com/PedroPovedaQ/Gaze-Contingency-Project}{the Gaze Contingency Project GitHub repository}. The following sections distinguish the documented implementation from the remaining requirements for the participant-study release.
 
+The methodological choices are informed by Chiossi et al.'s visual-search study, which combines search outcomes with eye-tracking measures~\cite{chiossi2024searching}. The present study adopts that separation between task performance and visual behavior while changing the research question to voice identity, withholding the target identity initially, and using a researcher-selected 15-second search limit. Complementary work by Pfeil et al. at UCF compares eye--head coordination in virtual and physical viewing~\cite{pfeil2018eyehead}; it motivates retaining head motion alongside eye direction when interpreting gaze in this application. These connections guide measurement design, rather than establish that detector parameters transfer unchanged between headsets and tasks.
+
 \section{Application Design}
 The application presents a virtual setup menu before the search task. Participants choose a neutral voice from the supplied options, record a standard passage for their self-similar voice, and confirm that both voices are clear and comfortable to hear. Voice preparation and eye-tracking setup precede practice. During search, participants remain seated on a swivel chair, inspect the surrounding scene, and use only the right controller for pointing and selection.
 
