@@ -96,6 +96,26 @@ A stopped or timed-out trial has an observed duration and an outcome reason, but
 
 Recent conversational AR research shows why the definition must be explicit: Madritsch et al. distinguish overall task-completion time from active working time in an assistant-supported procedural task~\cite{madritsch2026assistance}, and their active measure is not assumed to correspond to the present clock. In Follow My Voice, active completion time includes ordinary waits for guidance between object reveal and correct selection, and only recorded pauses are excluded. Separate decision and playback timestamps will therefore help explain delivery delays without removing their task-level consequences from the primary outcome.
 
+\subsection{Missed Targets}
+A missed target is a search that reaches its recorded 15-second active-search deadline without a correct trigger selection. An incorrect selection followed by a correct selection before expiry is a completed search, with its selection error retained separately. Technical stops, withdrawal, and recordings without a known terminal outcome are not automatically classified as misses. For each participant, block, voice condition, and recorded deadline, the descriptive missed-target percentage is
+\begin{equation}
+M=100\frac{N_{\mathrm{timeout}}}{N_{\mathrm{selected}}+N_{\mathrm{timeout}}}.
+\end{equation}
+The denominator excludes practice and unresolved or technically interrupted attempts; their counts are reported alongside the percentage. A zero denominator yields an unavailable percentage. The full trial export implements \texttt{missed\_target} as 1 for timeout, 0 for successful completion, and blank otherwise. A separate \texttt{missed\_targets.csv} records grouped counts, deadlines, and development-data exclusion labels. Legacy records without an explicit deadline are retained but excluded from this deadline-specific percentage.
+
+This outcome follows Chiossi et al.'s distinction between successful search and searches ending without selection~\cite{chiossi2024searching}. Their five-second task and the present 15-second, verbally guided task differ; percentages are therefore interpretable within each protocol and are not directly comparable benchmarks. The six unfinished searches in the earlier development recordings remain unfinished records, not retrospectively assigned missed targets.
+
+\subsection{Exploratory Eye-Event and Pupil Measures}
+An implemented offline analysis preserves per-eye directions and pupil diameters from the HTC query stream, deduplicates repeated query timestamps, and separates search from readiness and pauses. Query receipts establish when software obtained data, not the sensor capture time or the independence of consecutive observations. Changes in trial or device, invalid tracking, reversed timestamps, and query gaps longer than 25 ms break continuity. Invalid per-eye observations and observed eye closure exclude nearby 200-ms intervals. These are explicit engineering screening rules, subject to validation on the study apparatus.
+
+Fixation candidates use an elapsed-time dispersion detector: the sum of horizontal and vertical angular ranges must remain within 1.8 degrees for at least 83 ms. This starting configuration follows the I-DT parameters reported by Chiossi et al.~\cite{chiossi2024searching}, within the detector family described by Salvucci and Goldberg~\cite{salvucci2000fixations}. Azimuth is unwrapped across the signed-angle boundary. The implementation retains event intervals and the duration of eligible segments; its counts are candidate classifications rather than validated fixations.
+
+Saccade candidates are computed separately from eye-in-head angular velocity, with a provisional 60-degree-per-second threshold, a duration of 20--100 ms, at least two sampled intervals, and observed lower-velocity samples on either side. Intervals with head speed above 20 degrees per second or unavailable head pose are excluded. The reported rate divides event count by retained low-head-speed duration. It therefore describes a restricted observation window, not all saccades during search. These restrictions reduce head-turn ambiguity but do not validate event identity or establish sensor-level eye--head synchronization.
+
+Exploratory Index of Pupillary Activity (IPA) follows the wavelet-based approach of Duchowski et al.~\cite{duchowski2018ipa}. Each eye is processed separately. Accepted pupil segments contain valid finite diameters, last at least two seconds, meet a median query-rate screen of 60 Hz, and pass an interval-regularity screen. Within each accepted segment, values are resampled at its median query interval; no interpolation crosses rejected gaps or invalid observations. Level-two sym16 wavelet detail coefficients yield thresholded modulus maxima, which are counted per retained second. The implementation records its thresholds, resampling, numerical tolerance, and nearest-neighbor boundary convention. IPA is unavailable when no segment qualifies; it is never filled with zero to represent missing measurements.
+
+Computational tests verify event boundaries, query deduplication, invalid-data handling, head-turn suppression, and wavelet calculations. They do not establish physiological validity. Independent event annotations, calibration checks, timing assessment, and held-out threshold evaluation remain necessary before fixation counts or saccade rates enter the participant analysis. Pupil-based interpretation additionally requires checks for luminance, gaze angle, blink artifacts, segment boundaries, and task-related confounds. IPA remains exploratory alongside NASA-TLX, rather than a validated estimate of workload in this apparatus. Final fixation duration on the target also remains unavailable until validated eye events can be joined to target areas of interest; the last ray contact is not a substitute.
+
 \subsection{Gaze Contact, Vicinity, Pointing, and Selection}
 Four event families are central to interpretation: a valid eye ray can intersect the target collider; a gaze direction can satisfy the larger target-vicinity rule; the right-controller ray can intersect the target; and a trigger press can select the target. These events can occur at different times and need not occur in that order in every recorded sample sequence.
 
